From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: WavJaby <WavJaby@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 15:43:13 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Intercept stubs through hidden aliases of their own symbols

The INIT_INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_* macros passed &name / name to
native_bridge_intercept_symbol. Both resolve through the GOT, so an
app library loaded before the stub library that defines the same name
(Oboe defines SL_IID_* read-only and DT_NEEDs libOpenSLES.so)
interposes them: the bridge then writes the host value into the app's
data (SIGSEGV when read-only, silent overwrite otherwise) and records
the app's function as the stub's exported entry.

Each stub variable and exported function now gets a hidden alias,
name##_nb_own, and the INIT macros pass it, so the address is the
stub library's own definition by construction. Exported symbols and
all other interposition are unchanged.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Patch-Reason: an app library exporting a stubbed name (liboboe SL_IID_*) interposes the stub's &name, so the constructor writes into the app's read-only data; the bridge receives only an address and cannot tell whose it is
---
SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 .../vdso/interceptable_functions.h            | 20 ++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/android_api/vdso/include/native_bridge_support/vdso/interceptable_functions.h b/android_api/vdso/include/native_bridge_support/vdso/interceptable_functions.h
index bffb1b2595a822960751bb90edf8c37abfff854e..a351ca8335e4eb5056b37c296bd91548445b8363 100644
--- a/android_api/vdso/include/native_bridge_support/vdso/interceptable_functions.h
+++ b/android_api/vdso/include/native_bridge_support/vdso/interceptable_functions.h
@@ -107,15 +107,25 @@
 
 #endif
 
-#define DEFINE_INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_VARIABLE(name) uintptr_t name;
+// The INIT macros pass a hidden alias (name##_nb_own), never the exported name itself: an app library
+// loaded before this one may define the same name (e.g. Oboe's SL_IID_*), and ELF interposition
+// would then bind &name to the app's copy, so the bridge would write into the app's (possibly
+// read-only) data. A hidden alias always resolves to this library's own definition. A variable
+// at namespace scope has an unmangled linkage name, so alias(#name) names it.
+
+#define DEFINE_INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_VARIABLE(name) \
+  uintptr_t name;                                \
+  extern uintptr_t name##_nb_own __attribute__((alias(#name), visibility("hidden")))
 
 #define INIT_INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_VARIABLE(library_name, name) \
-  native_bridge_intercept_symbol(&name, library_name, #name)
+  native_bridge_intercept_symbol(&name##_nb_own, library_name, #name)
 
-#define DEFINE_INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_FUNCTION(name) INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_ASM_FUNCTION(name)
+#define DEFINE_INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_FUNCTION(name) \
+  INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_ASM_FUNCTION(name)          \
+  extern "C" void name##_nb_own() __attribute__((alias(#name), visibility("hidden")))
 
-#define INIT_INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_FUNCTION(library_name, name)                          \
-  native_bridge_intercept_symbol(reinterpret_cast<void*>(name), library_name, #name); \
+#define INIT_INTERCEPTABLE_STUB_FUNCTION(library_name, name)                                \
+  native_bridge_intercept_symbol(reinterpret_cast<void*>(name##_nb_own), library_name, #name); \
   native_bridge_intercept_symbol(reinterpret_cast<void*>(name##_stub), library_name, #name)
 
 #endif  // NATIVE_BRIDGE_SUPPORT_VDSO_INTERCEPTABLE_FUNCTIONS_H_
